            <ArticleTitle>Management of peripheral neuropathy in diabetes mellitus. Recent research findings and their therapeutic implications.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>183-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Peripheral neuropathy in diabetes begins as a physiologic aberration related to hyperglycemia and its subsequent effects of endoneurial hypoxia, elevated sorbitol levels, and decreased myoinositol levels. Resultant decreases in sodium-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase levels ultimately lead to structural alterations at the nodes of Ranvier. Aldose reductase inhibitors and dietary myoinositol supplementation are being used in long-term clinical studies to monitor the possibility that they may prevent or reverse these abnormalities. In the meantime, symptomatic treatment remains the mainstay of management.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
